    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65399",
      "knowledge_point_id": "141d61c7-1d82-52fe-b977-19daa910da9b",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring Alternative Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify ${sec}^{4}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we can simplify our expression by factoring it as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{4}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x & =({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)({sec}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)({sec}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{2}⁡x) & =(1)({sec}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
