    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65400",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9b14ef98-8122-512a-9cce-6e44392959d8",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity",
      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to ${sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{4}⁡\\theta +2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{4}⁡\\theta +2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 & =-{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1)+2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression using $(2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{4}⁡\\theta +2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$",
