    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-74157",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9b14ef98-8122-512a-9cce-6e44392959d8",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity",
      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to ${sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x-1) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x & =(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x){tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x=(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x){tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$",
