    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-74162",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9b14ef98-8122-512a-9cce-6e44392959d8",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity",
      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to ${sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$",
